## conversation guide

⭐ **lines for YOU to say — and that is why they are fenced rather than
block-quoted.** a `>` block in this route means *"someone published these words
and you can check them against bytes on disk."* speech you have not uttered yet
has no publisher and never will, so a `>` block would file it alongside the
statutes and send `quote.verify` hunting for a source that cannot exist.
⚠️ **do not "tidy" this back into a block quote.**

```text
"Hi, I'd like to request a recalculation of back taxes for a void
 corporation using the Assumed Par Value Method."

"The file number is [NUMBER]."

"The corporation name is [NAME]."

"For tax year [YEAR], total assets on Schedule L were $[AMOUNT] and
 issued shares were [NUMBER]."

"Can you recalculate the tax owed using Assumed Par Value?"
```

⛔ **say the figure, not an approximation of it.** that line used to read *"gross
assets were **approximately** $[AMOUNT]"* — but 8 Del. C. § 503(i) defines gross
assets as the *"total assets" reported to the United States on U.S. Form 1120
Schedule L*, and the state will ask for that signed return to substantiate it. an
approximation invites a second call and a discrepancy of your own creation.

⚠️ if you do not have the figure from Schedule L, you are not ready for this
call — go back to step 0.

---

## what to ask

### ⛔ ask this FIRST — every question below presupposes its answer

**ask, in these words — "which tax years does the account currently show as
delinquent?"**

⚠️ **you probably do not know this, and you probably think you do.** the count
is easy to derive wrongly from either end:

| you reason from | you get |
|---|---|
| the **void date** — "void since March 2024, so 2024 and 2025 too" | the larger set |
| the **assessed total** ÷ the per-year figure | whatever divides evenly |

⭐ **those two can disagree, and when they do the second is the better witness**
— it is arithmetic on a number Delaware actually sent you. but it is still a
**fit**, never a statement: a residual that "looks like penalty and interest"
is a hypothesis about the State's books.

⇒ the year set multiplies through every figure and every step:

```
tax        = per-year amount  ×  YEARS
penalty    = $200             ×  YEARS      (§502(c))
reports    = one amendment    ×  YEARS      (§503(b), each a fee + a round trip)
interest   = per-month, from EACH year's own due dates  (§504(c))
```

⛔ **a wrong year count does not make the total slightly wrong — it makes the
WORK PLAN wrong**, because step 2 submits one amended report per year and they
must be serialized. two extra years is two extra submissions, two extra fees,
and two more chances for a returned document.

🚩 **and write down which years, not just how many.** "four years" cannot be
acted on; "2022, 2023, 2024, 2025" can. the per-year rows below depend on it.

#### ⭐ but do NOT call the whole year set open — §312(g) fixes HALF of it

**two different year sets hide under one word, and only one of them is a
question for the clerk.** the statute settles the other:

> "Any corporation that revives its certificate of incorporation under this
> chapter shall pay to this State a sum equal to all franchise taxes, penalties
> and interest thereon **due at the time its certificate of incorporation became
> forfeited or void** pursuant to this title"
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 312(g),
> https://delcode.delaware.gov/title8/c001/sc12/index.html

**"due at the time … became void"** makes the revival payment a **snapshot**,
taken on the void date. a year whose March 1 had not yet arrived on that date
was not due then, so §312(g) does not reach it — however many years the account
has carried since.

| question | who answers it |
|---|---|
| which years must be paid **to revive**? | ⭐ **the statute** — every year already due on the void date |
| which years does the **account** carry today? | the clerk, on this call — Chapter 5 accrues annually |
| is the **revival year itself** covered by that payment? | ⛔ no. §312(g)'s last sentence says so in terms |

⇒ so ask the clerk for the **ledger**, and expect it to be the larger set. do
not let a larger ledger talk you into a larger revival payment, and do not let
the §312(g) snapshot talk you out of a tax the account still shows.

⚠️ **the snapshot rule has a cliff at five years.** the same subsection swaps it
for *"a sum equal to 3 times the amount of the annual franchise tax that would be
due and payable … for the year in which the revival is effected"* once the
charter has been void **more than 5 years**. check your void date against that
line before you compute a figure — past the cliff, the back years no longer set
the revival sum; the current year's rate sets it instead.

---

1. what is the recalculated tax for each year?
2. what is the total penalty and interest?
3. **is there an annual report fee for each back year, and how much?**
4. what is the total amount owed?
5. can I pay by credit card or do I need ACH?
6. what is the reference number for this recalculation?

### ⭐ the § 503(b) questions — ask these PER YEAR, or the election may not land

7. **which years already have a report on file?** a year with one takes an
   **amended** report; a year with none takes an **original**. a wrong
   submission costs a fee and a round trip.
8. 🚨 **which fiscal year's Form 1120 Schedule L does each back-year report
   take?** ⚠️ see the retraction below — this question replaces an earlier one
   that read § 503(b) without § 503(i), and got the answer backwards.

   ✅ **this is no longer open-ended — put BOTH texts to them.**
   the Division's own calculator page anchors gross assets to *"the company’s
   fiscal year ending the calendar year of the report"*, which points a 2023
   report at fiscal year **2023**. § 503(i) anchors to the calendar year prior
   to when you file, which points the same report at fiscal year **2025**. so
   ask it with the citation attached — **say this**:

   **"your calculate-franchise-taxes page says the fiscal year of the report.
   § 503(i) says the year prior to when I file. for an amended 2023 report
   submitted in 2026, which Schedule L do you want?"**

   ⇒ a 20-second question with an authority behind each option, rather than an
   open one that invites a guess. the full read is in `0.gather.position.stone`;
   the pulled bytes are at
   `briefs/incorporation/variant=c-corp/refs/pulledAt=2026-08-26.de-frtaxcalc.txt`.
9. **do you need issued shares broken out per class?** § 503(b) asks for *"the
   number of shares of each class of stock actually issued, **if any**"* —
   ⚠️ **issued**, never authorized, and zero is a valid answer the statute names.
10. **will the portal expose an amended report for a tax year whose charter
    stood void?** ⚠️ **ask this narrowly.** the Division has already confirmed
    you may file back-year amendments **while void** (see the retraction at the
    head of this stone) — so do not spend the call on that. what is open is the
    years **inside** the void window, which is a different question and a
    different answer.

11. 🚨 **has a Governor's proclamation under § 511 issued that names this file
    number, and on what date?** ⚠️ ask it as a **records lookup**, never as a
    legal question — see the section below for the exact phrasing and for why it
    belongs on this call.
12. 🚨 **what signature does the office accept on the Certificate of Renewal —
    wet ink, a scan of wet ink, or a digital signature?** ⛔ § 116(b)(1) pulls
    *"A document filed with or submitted to the Secretary of State"* out of the
    DGCL's electronic-signature safe harbor, **and expressly creates no
    presumption either way** — so the statute routes this question to the office
    rather than answers it. ⚠️ do **not** infer it from the fact that the
    Division accepted a signed PDF by email: acceptance of a **substantiation
    document** is not acceptance of a **filed certificate**. the full read is in
    `4.file.revival.stone`.
13. 🚨 **for each back year, what figure anchors the § 504(e) tentative-tax
    estimate — the assessed method-A figure, or the recalculated one?** and
    **what was the annual franchise tax of record for the year before the first
    delinquent year?** ⭐ a money question, not a formality: § 504(e) keys the
    quarterly regime to *"the annual franchise tax of the preceding year"*, so if
    the anchor is the recalculated figure, **no tentative return was ever due**
    and § 504(c) interest runs from March 1 only — instead of from June 1,
    September 1 and December 1 of each tax year. ⚠️ ask for the prior year's
    figure **as it sits on their record**; it predates the void and is not in
    our files. the full read is in `1.calculate.owed.stone`.
14. 🚨 **(only if a year's federal return is not yet on file) what will you
    accept for gross assets on that year's amended report?** ⭐ **lead with the fact that it cannot
    move the money**, because that reframes the whole ask — **say this**:

    **"under the assumed-par method we land on the $400.00 minimum across the
    entire plausible range of gross assets — the figure cannot change the tax.
    the federal return for that year is not filed yet. what will you accept on
    the report in the meantime?"**

    ⚠️ **this is a FORM gate, never a MONEY gate**, and the two have different
    cures. § 503(b) demands a figure on the report; § 503(a)(2) computes a tax
    that sits on the floor whatever the figure is. so the question is what
    satisfies the **form** — a good-faith estimate, a zero with a note, an
    amendment once the return lands, or a wait.

    ⛔ do **not** put a number on a sworn report to unblock yourself. § 502(b)
    is under penalty of perjury and § 504(d) gives inquiry power over *"the
    truth or falsity or accuracy of every report."* ask, then do what they say.

### 🛑 RETRACTED — question 8 read the GRANT and missed the LIMIT

> **the question 8 above replaces this one, retracted:**
>
> > ~~8. **what as-of date do you want for gross assets, per year?** § 503(b)
> > says *"as of the nearest date on which the amount is obtainable"* — it is
> > not a strict fiscal-year-end rule, so ask what they will accept rather than
> > assume.~~

the quote was accurate and the conclusion drawn from it was **backwards**, because
§ 503(b) is only half the provision. read the two together:

> "(b) Unless a corporation shall submit to the Secretary of State, at the time
> of filing its annual franchise tax report, a statement setting forth the number
> of shares of each class of stock actually issued, if any, and the amount of the
> total gross assets of the corporation, **as of the nearest date on which the
> amount is obtainable** ..."
>
> "**(i) As used in subsections (a) and (b) of this section** … Such total assets
> and total gross assets **shall be** those 'total assets' reported to the United
> States on U.S. Form 1120 Schedule L, relative to the company's fiscal year
> ending in the calendar year prior to **filing** with the Secretary of State
> pursuant to this section."
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 503(b), § 503(i)

| the half you read | what it appears to say |
|---|---|
| **(b) alone** — the grant | a soft standard: whatever date the figure is obtainable on |
| ⭐ **(b) + (i)** — the limit | **"shall be"** one named page of one named return, for one named fiscal year |

⭐ **(i) scopes itself onto (b) in its own first words**, so this is not an
inference — the statute says which subsections it governs. a question built on
(b) alone invites the clerk to bless a date the statute does not leave open, and
you would file a sworn report on it.

🎯 **so the live ambiguity is not "which date" — it is "which FISCAL YEAR."**
(i) keys on the **filing**, and a delinquent filer's filing year and report year
diverge by however many years they are behind. that is the question now on the
list, and `0.gather.position.stone` carries the worked table.

⚠️ **the tell, for next time:** sentence one granted, sentence two limited, and
only sentence one was quoted. when a provision reads permissive, look for the
subsection that names it by letter.

### 🚨 the § 511 question — ask it, because § 513 carries a CRIMINAL penalty

§ 510 voids the charter, and that is where most readers stop. a **separate**
ladder runs beside it and ends somewhere else entirely:

> "On or before **June 30** in each year, the Secretary of State shall report to
> the Governor a list of all the corporations, which for **1 year** next
> preceding such report, have failed, neglected or refused to pay the franchise
> taxes assessed against them or due by them, **or to file a complete annual
> franchise tax report** ..., and the Governor shall forthwith issue a
> **proclamation declaring that the charters of these corporations are
> repealed**."
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 511, https://delcode.delaware.gov/title8/c005/index.html

> "Whoever exercises or attempts to exercise any powers under the certificate of
> incorporation of any corporation **which has been proclaimed by the Governor,
> after the issuance of the proclamation**, shall be **fined not more than
> $1,000 or imprisoned not more than 1 year, or both**."
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 513

⛔ **read what § 513 attaches to.** not the void — the **proclamation**. and not
the corporation — *"whoever"*, which reaches the human who acted. so a
delinquent corporation that kept up its business has a civil problem under § 510
and may have a **criminal** one under § 513, and the two turn on different facts
and different dates.

⭐ **§ 512 is what makes this a cheap question rather than an alarming one.** the
list *"shall be filed in the office of the Secretary of State"*, and the
proclamation is published each year. **whether your corporation is on it is a
records lookup the person on the phone can perform** — not an opinion, not
advice, and not a matter they will decline to answer.

🎯 **phrase it exactly that way:**

```text
"Can you check whether a Governor's proclamation under section 511
 lists file number [NUMBER]? And if it does, what date did that
 proclamation issue?"
```

⚠️ fenced, not block-quoted — same reason as the conversation guide above: these
are words **you** will say, so there is no publisher to check them against.

⚠️ **what you do with the answer is not a phone question.** § 513 is a criminal
statute. if a proclamation names the corporation and business continued after
its date, take that to a licensed attorney.

### ⭐ the SAME answer closes a second door — § 509 and the receiver

the proclamation is not only the trigger of a criminal penalty. it is also a
**precondition** of a receiver, and that makes one records question do two jobs:

> "(a) After any corporation ... **has failed or neglected for the period of 1
> year to pay the franchise taxes** imposed by law, **and the Secretary of State
> shall have reported such corporation to the Governor** of the State, as
> provided in **§ 511** of this title, **then the Attorney General** of this
> State **may proceed against the corporation in the Court of Chancery** of this
> State **for the appointment of a receiver**, or otherwise."
>
> — 8 Del. C. § 509(a), https://delcode.delaware.gov/title8/c005/index.html

⛔ **read the conjunction.** § 509(a) needs **both** — a year of unpaid franchise
tax **and** the § 511 report. a delinquent corporation satisfies the first by
definition, so the § 511 answer is the whole question.

| the answer to question 11 | what it settles |
|---|---|
| **no proclamation names the file** | ⛔ § 513 has no purchase, and § 509(a)'s second condition is unmet |
| **a proclamation issued on [DATE]** | ⚠️ both doors stand open as of that date |

⭐ **a receiver is the one outcome in this route that takes the matter out of
your hands entirely** — the Court appoints, the Court ascertains the amount, and
§ 509(b) issues process to collect it. so the § 511 answer is not trivia; it
tells you how much time pressure you are actually under.

🎯 **and it argues for the order this route already takes.** the taxes you pay at
step 3 remove § 509(a)'s **first** condition. ⚠️ whether an action already
commenced is affected by a later payment or a later revival is **a question for
counsel**, not for this stone.

🚩 **and one question stays open here, stated bare.** § 312(e) validates
*"all contracts, acts, matters and things"* done while the charter was void, as
if it had never lapsed. **whether that reaches a § 513 penalty is unestablished**
— this route does not answer it, and a guess in this cell would close a gate that
should stay open.

⛔ **questions 7-9 are not paperwork.** § 503(b) closes with *"it shall pay a
franchise tax for such year computed in the manner prescribed by paragraph
(a)(1)"* — so an incomplete statement does not reject, it routes that year back
to Authorized Shares **silently**. there is no error message to catch it later.

⚠️ **question 3 is the one the tools cannot answer.** `franchise.tax.delaware`
prints `⬅️ tax only` and `.accrue` returns penalty and interest — neither covers
the report fee, and § 510 voids on an unfiled complete annual report
independently of the tax, so a report is owed for every delinquent year. the
current-year fee is $50 non-exempt / $25 exempt; **whether the same applies to a
back report is unestablished**, which is why it is asked rather than assumed.

